<DocsLayout
  title="Modes — Golem Docs"
  description="Clay, Stone and Rune: what each Golem mode does, what it costs in Sparks, and how to choose the right one for the job."
  heading="Modes: Clay, Stone, Rune"
>
  <p>
    Every message you send wakes one of three golems. They differ in how much autonomy they take
    and how much work they do per request — and therefore in what they cost. The craft of using
    Golem well is mostly picking the smallest golem that can carry the job.
  </p>

  <h2>Clay — 1 spark</h2>
  <p><em>Fast answers and small edits.</em></p>
  <p>
    Clay is conversational and quick. It reads the parts of your project that matter to your
    question and responds in seconds. Use Clay for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>“What does this script do?” / “Why is this deprecated?”</li>
    <li>Single-script edits: tweak a value, rename things, fix an obvious bug.</li>
    <li>Roblox API questions with your project as context.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    At 1 spark per request, you can lean on Clay all day. It is also the best way to
    <em>plan</em> a big build cheaply before handing the plan to a bigger golem.
  </p>

  <h2>Stone — 4 sparks</h2>
  <p><em>Builds features across your project.</em></p>
  <p>
    Stone is the everyday builder. It reads your project tree, edits multiple scripts, creates
    and wires instances, and reports what it did. Use Stone for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Add a shop that sells speed boosts for coins.”</li>
    <li>“Make a checkpoint system for the obby.”</li>
    <li>Refactors that touch several scripts at once.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Stone finishes a request in one pass and then stops — it does not playtest on its own. Press
    Play, see what you think, and tell it what to change.
  </p>

  <h2>Rune — 10 sparks, +1 per extra step</h2>
  <p><em>Plans, builds, tests and fixes autonomously.</em></p>
  <p>
    Rune is the deep agent. Give it an outcome, and it writes a plan, executes it step by step,
    runs your game in Studio, reads the output, and repairs what breaks — repeating until the
    outcome holds or it needs your input. Use Rune for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Build a complete round system: intermission, map vote, teleport, win detection.”</li>
    <li>“Something is breaking coins on death — find it and fix it, prove it with a run.”</li>
    <li>Anything where “done” means <em>verified working</em>, not <em>code written</em>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Rune requests start at 10 sparks and add <strong>1 spark per extra step</strong> beyond the
    included ones. The running cost is displayed live during the run, and you can stop a Rune run
    at any moment — completed steps stay applied (and undoable).
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--violet">
    <p>
      <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> question → Clay. Feature → Stone. Outcome you want proven →
      Rune. When unsure, start one size smaller; escalating costs you nothing but the retry.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Switching modes</h2>
  <p>
    The mode picker sits next to the message box. Every message can use a different mode — a
    common rhythm is Clay to plan, Rune to build, Clay to ask “what did you just do?”, Stone to
    polish. All modes share the same project memory, so nothing is lost between switches.
  </p>

  <h2>Costs at a glance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clay</strong> — 1 spark per request</li>
    <li><strong>Stone</strong> — 4 sparks per request</li>
    <li><strong>Rune</strong> — 10 sparks per request, +1 per extra agent step</li>
  </ul>
  <p>See <a href="/docs/sparks-and-limits">Sparks &amp; limits</a> for how the daily quota works.</p>
</DocsLayout>
